Finding 8: TCP Timestamps Information Disclosure
Severity
Low
CVSS
2.6

Affected Service
Service: TCP/IP Stack
Affected Ports: Multiple

Description
OpenVAS identified that the target system has TCP timestamps enabled, allowing it to disclose timing information during TCP communications. While TCP timestamps are commonly used to improve network performance and calculate round-trip times, 
they may inadvertently reveal information about the system's uptime and internal clock, providing attackers with additional reconnaissance data.

Business Impact
Although this vulnerability does not directly enable system compromise, the disclosed timing information can assist attackers during the reconnaissance phase of an attack. 
System uptime and timestamp values may be used to fingerprint operating systems, estimate reboot schedules, or support more advanced attack techniques by providing additional intelligence about the target environment.

Validation
The vulnerability was identified through OpenVAS network analysis and confirmed by observing TCP timestamp values returned by the target during the TCP handshake. 
The affected behavior was present across multiple network services exposed by the host.
nmap -O <Target-IP>
The scan confirmed the target's TCP/IP stack characteristics, supporting the OpenVAS assessment.

Recommendation
Disable TCP timestamps where operationally feasible, particularly on internet-facing systems.
Review operating system network stack configurations to minimize unnecessary information disclosure.
Implement network hardening measures that reduce host fingerprinting opportunities.
Regularly assess exposed services to identify configuration weaknesses that may aid reconnaissance activities.
